        <h2 class="faq-category">The Process & What to Expect</h2>
        
        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How do I know if it is the right time to euthanize my pet?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">Knowing when it's time is often the hardest part. We recommend evaluating your pet's 'Quality of Life,' looking at factors like their ability to eat, drink, walk, their pain levels, and whether they have more bad days than good ones. Our veterinarians also offer quality-of-life consultations to help guide your decision.</p>
        </article>
        
        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How does the in-home euthanasia process work?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">First, the veterinarian will arrive and gently introduce themselves to your pet. Once you are ready, a calming sedative is given so your pet falls into a deep, painless sleep. Finally, the euthanasia medication is administered, allowing them to pass peacefully within moments.</p>
        </article>
        
        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Will my pet feel any pain during the procedure?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">No. We always administer a heavy sedative before the final medication. The initial sedative injection may feel like a tiny pinch (similar to a vaccine), but once it takes effect, your pet is completely asleep and unaware of the final injection.</p>
        </article>
        
        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How long does the home visit take?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">A typical visit lasts between 45 to 60 minutes. We do not rush the process; we move at a pace that is comfortable for you and your pet.</p>
        </article>

        <h2 class="faq-category">Preparing Your Family & Home</h2>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Should other pets be present?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">Yes, we encourage allowing other household pets to be present or at least allowed to sniff their friend after they have passed. This helps them understand what happened and process their own grief.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How should I prepare my home for the visit?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">Choose a spot where your pet is most comfortable, such as their favorite bed, the couch, or even out in the yard if the weather is nice. You may want to provide some low lighting, soft music, and have their favorite treats or toys nearby.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Can children be present?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">Yes. Being at home allows children to grieve openly in a safe environment. We recommend explaining the process to them beforehand in simple, age-appropriate terms. If a child decides they want to leave the room, that is perfectly okay too.</p>
        </article>

        <h2 class="faq-category">Aftercare (Cremation & Burial)</h2>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">What happens after my pet passes away?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">If you choose cremation with us, the doctor will respectfully carry your pet to their vehicle. You can choose Private Cremation (ashes returned to you in an urn) or Communal Cremation (ashes scattered at a memorial site). If you prefer a home burial, we will leave your pet with you.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Are there laws against home burial?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">Regulations regarding home burial vary widely by city and county in California. Generally, you must own the property and bury the pet sufficiently deep. We recommend checking your local ordinances before planning a home burial.</p>
        </article>

        <h2 class="faq-category">Costs & Payment</h2>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How much does in home pet euthanasia cost?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">The cost varies depending on your pet's size (weight) and your location. The base cost for the visit, sedation, and euthanasia typically ranges from $300 to $450+. Aftercare (cremation) is an additional cost based on the option you choose. Please view our <a href="/pricing.html">Pricing page</a> for details.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">When and how do I pay?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">To minimize stress during the visit, we prefer to handle payment over the phone prior to the appointment. We accept all major credit/debit cards.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Do you offer emergency or after-hours appointments?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">Yes, we strive to accommodate urgent, same-day requests and after-hours appointments, subject to veterinarian availability. Additional fees may apply for evenings, weekends, or holidays.</p>
        </article>


        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Do you provide at home euthanasia services for dogs and cats?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">Yes, our mobile veterinarians specialize in providing peaceful at home euthanasia services for both dogs and cats. We understand the unique needs of different species and adjust our gentle approach accordingly to ensure a stress-free passing.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Are there options for free dog euthanasia in Los Angeles?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">As a private mobile veterinary practice, we do not offer free dog euthanasia services. However, if you are facing severe financial hardship, we recommend reaching out to local non-profit animal shelters or humane societies in Los Angeles, as they sometimes offer low-cost or free compassionate services at their clinics.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How quickly can I schedule mobile euthanasia near me?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">We strive to accommodate families as quickly as possible. We often have same-day appointments available for urgent needs. Please call us directly, and we will do our best to dispatch a veterinarian to your location promptly.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Do you offer pet cremation in Fontana, Hesperia, and Redlands?</h3>
          <p class="faq-answer">Yes! We offer respectful pet cremation services (both private and communal) across our entire service area, including Fontana, Hesperia, Redlands, and the wider Southern California region.</p>
        </article>
